{"task_id": "Mbpp/235", "solution": "def even_bit_set_number(number: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Sets all even-indexed bits (0-indexed from the right) of a given integer to 1,\n    preserving the state of its odd-indexed bits.\n    \"\"\"\n    modified_number = number\n    # Iterate through bit positions up to the effective length of the number's\n    # binary representation. For Python's arbitrary-precision integers, this\n    # covers all significant bits of the number.\n    # For 0, bit_length() is 0, so the loop correctly does not run.\n    max_bit_length = number.bit_length()\n    if number < 0:\n        # For negative numbers, bit_length() works differently.\n        # We need to consider the number of bits for its absolute value.\n        max_bit_length = abs(number).bit_length()\n\n    for bit_position in range(max_bit_length):\n        # If the bit position is even, set that bit to 1 using a bitmask and OR operation.\n        if bit_position % 2 == 0:\n            bit_mask = 1 << bit_position\n            modified_number = modified_number | bit_mask\n            \n    return modified_number"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/237", "solution": "def check_occurences(tuple_list: list[tuple]) -> dict[tuple, int]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Counts occurrences of canonical tuples in a list of tuples.\n\n    Returns a dictionary mapping unique canonical tuples to their occurrence counts.\n    \"\"\"\n    counts = {}\n    for tpl in tuple_list:\n        # Convert tuple to its canonical form (sorted elements as a new tuple)\n        canonical_tuple = tuple(sorted(tpl))\n        counts[canonical_tuple] = counts.get(canonical_tuple, 0) + 1\n    return counts"}
