{"task_id": "Mbpp/306", "solution": "def max_sum_increasing_subseq(sequence: list[int | float], n: int, i: int, k: int) -> int | float:\n    # Validate inputs as per design context, although explicit checks for isinstance() are excluded.\n    # The current problem statement and assertion implicitly handle basic valid cases.\n\n    # Dynamic programming approach\n    # max_sum_ending_at_j[x] stores the maximum sum of an increasing subsequence ending at index x,\n    # using elements from sequence[0...x].\n    max_sum_ending_at_j = [sequence[j] for j in range(n)]\n\n    for current_idx in range(i + 1):\n        for prev_idx in range(current_idx):\n            if sequence[current_idx] > sequence[prev_idx]:\n                max_sum_ending_at_j[current_idx] = max(max_sum_ending_at_j[current_idx],\n                                                       sequence[current_idx] + max_sum_ending_at_j[prev_idx])\n\n    # Step 2: Find the maximum sum of an increasing subsequence ending at any index j <= i,\n    # such that sequence[j] < sequence[k].\n    max_sum_before_k = 0\n    # Initialize with a value that ensures sequence[k] is added even if no valid prefix exists.\n    # The problem implies finding a sum, so sequence[k] is the minimum valid sum if no prefix works.\n    \n    found_valid_prefix = False\n    for j in range(i + 1):\n        if sequence[j] < sequence[k]:\n            max_sum_before_k = max(max_sum_before_k, max_sum_ending_at_j[j])\n            found_valid_prefix = True\n\n    # The total sum must include sequence[k].\n    # If no element before k can start an increasing subsequence with sequence[k],\n    # the subsequence is just sequence[k] itself.\n    if not found_valid_prefix:\n        return sequence[k]\n\n    return max_sum_before_k + sequence[k]"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/305", "solution": "def start_withp(input_list: list[str]) -> tuple[str, str]:\n    \"\"\"Returns the first two words from a list of strings that start with 'p' or 'P'.\"\"\"\n\n    def _extract_words_from_string(text: str) -> list[str]:\n        \"\"\"Splits a string into words based on whitespace.\"\"\"\n        return text.split()\n\n    def _is_p_word(word: str) -> bool:\n        \"\"\"Checks if a word starts with 'p' or 'P' (case-insensitive).\"\"\"\n        return word.lower().startswith('p')\n\n    p_words_found = []\n    for s in input_list:\n        words = _extract_words_from_string(s)\n        for word in words:\n            if _is_p_word(word):\n                p_words_found.append(word)\n                if len(p_words_found) == 2:\n                    return tuple(p_words_found)\n    return tuple(p_words_found)"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/308", "solution": "import itertools\n\ndef large_product(list1: list[float | int], list2: list[float | int], k: int) -> list[float | int]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Computes and returns the 'k' largest products from two input lists, sorted in descending order.\n    It generates all possible products, sorts them, and then selects the top 'k'.\n    \"\"\"\n    all_products = []\n    for num1, num2 in itertools.product(list1, list2):\n        all_products.append(num1 * num2)\n\n    all_products.sort(reverse=True)\n\n    return all_products[:k]"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/311", "solution": "```python\ndef set_left_most_unset_bit(n: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"Sets the left-most (most significant) unset bit of a non-negative integer.\n\n    Raises ValueError if n is negative.\n    \"\"\"\n    if n < 0:\n        raise ValueError(\"Input integer must be non-negative.\")\n\n    if n == 0:\n        return 1\n\n    # Iterate from the bit length downwards to find the most significant unset bit\n    # n.bit_length() gives the number of bits required to represent n,\n    # so the highest bit position is n.bit_length() - 1.\n    # We check one position higher as well, in case n is all ones (e.g., 3 (0b11) -> 7 (0b111)).\n    for p in range(n.bit_length(), -1, -1):\n        # Check if the bit at position p is 0\n        if (n >> p) & 1 == 0:\n            # Found the left-most unset bit, create a mask and set it\n            return n | (1 << p)\n    \n    # This case should ideally not be reached for non-negative integers\n    # unless n has an infinite number of bits, which is not possible.\n    # If the loop finishes, it means n was all 1s up to the highest checked bit,\n    # and the next bit position (p=0) was also 1, implying all bits are set.\n    # However, Python integers have arbitrary precision, so there's always an unset bit at higher positions.\n    # The loop condition `range(n.bit_length(), -1, -1)` correctly covers this by looking at\n    # `n.bit_length()` which would be the first bit position beyond the current highest set bit.\n    # For example, if n = 3 (0b11), n.bit_length() is 2. The loop starts at p=2.\n    # (3 >> 2) & 1 == (0b011 >> 2) & 1 == 0b000 & 1 == 0. So it finds p=2 and returns 3 | (1<<2) = 3 | 4 = 7.\n    # The current logic implicitly handles cases where n consists of all set bits up to its bit_length().\n    # An example is n=7 (0b111). n.bit_length() is 3. Loop starts with p=3.\n    # (7 >> 3) & 1 == (0b111 >> 3) & 1 == 0b000 & 1 == 0. It finds p=3 and returns 7 | (1<<3) = 7 | 8 = 15.\n    \n    return n\n```"}
