{"task_id": "Mbpp/445", "solution": "def index_multiplication(tuple1: tuple, tuple2: tuple) -> tuple:\n    \"\"\"\n    Performs index-wise multiplication of elements in two input tuples.\n    It validates inputs for correctness and structure before performing the multiplication.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not isinstance(tuple1, tuple) or not isinstance(tuple2, tuple):\n        raise TypeError(\"Both inputs must be tuples.\")\n\n    if len(tuple1) != len(tuple2):\n        raise ValueError(\"Input tuples must have the same number of nested tuples.\")\n\n    result_list = []\n    for nested_tuple1, nested_tuple2 in zip(tuple1, tuple2):\n        if not isinstance(nested_tuple1, tuple) or not isinstance(nested_tuple2, tuple):\n            raise TypeError(\"All elements within the main tuples must be nested tuples.\")\n\n        if len(nested_tuple1) != len(nested_tuple2):\n            raise ValueError(\"Nested tuples must have the same number of elements.\")\n\n        inner_result_list = []\n        for e1, e2 in zip(nested_tuple1, nested_tuple2):\n            if not isinstance(e1, (int, float)) or not isinstance(e2, (int, float)):\n                raise TypeError(\"All elements within nested tuples must be numerical (int or float).\")\n            inner_result_list.append(e1 * e2)\n        result_list.append(tuple(inner_result_list))\n\n    return tuple(result_list)"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/440", "solution": "import string\n\ndef _strip_punctuation(word_with_punct: str) -> str:\n    \"\"\"Strips leading and trailing punctuation from a word.\"\"\"\n    return word_with_punct.strip(string.punctuation)\n\ndef _is_adverb(stripped_word: str) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"Checks if a stripped word is likely an adverb using a suffix and a common list.\"\"\"\n    # Common '-ly' adverbs\n    if len(stripped_word) > 2 and stripped_word.endswith('ly'):\n        return True\n    # A small set of common non-'-ly' adverbs\n    common_adverbs = {\n        \"always\", \"never\", \"often\", \"seldom\", \"sometimes\", \"usually\", \"rarely\",\n        \"here\", \"there\", \"everywhere\", \"nowhere\", \"somewhere\",\n        \"today\", \"yesterday\", \"tomorrow\", \"now\", \"then\", \"soon\", \"early\", \"late\",\n        \"very\", \"quite\", \"rather\", \"too\", \"enough\", \"hardly\", \"almost\", \"just\",\n        \"well\", \"fast\",\n    }\n    return stripped_word.lower() in common_adverbs\n\ndef find_adverb_position(sentence: str) -> tuple[int, int, str]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the first adverb and its original positions in a given sentence.\n    Returns (start_index, end_index, stripped_adverb) or (-1, -1, '') if no adverb is found.\n    \"\"\"\n    current_pos = 0\n    while current_pos < len(sentence):\n        # Skip leading whitespace to find the start of a word\n        while current_pos < len(sentence) and sentence[current_pos].isspace():\n            current_pos += 1\n\n        if current_pos >= len(sentence):\n            break # Reached end of sentence or only spaces were left\n\n        word_start = current_pos\n        word_end = current_pos\n        # Define a 'word' as a sequence of non-whitespace characters\n        while word_end < len(sentence) and not sentence[word_end].isspace():\n            word_end += 1\n\n        original_word = sentence[word_start:word_end]\n        stripped_word = _strip_punctuation(original_word)\n\n        if _is_adverb(stripped_word):\n            return (word_start, word_end, stripped_word)\n\n        current_pos = word_end # Move to the character immediately after the current word\n\n    return (-1, -1, '')"}
