{"task_id": "Mbpp/468", "solution": "def max_product(arr: list[int]) -> int:\n    \"\"\"Calculates the maximum product of an increasing subsequence in the array.\"\"\"\n\n    def _calculate_product(subsequence: list[int]) -> int:\n        \"\"\"Calculates the product of all elements in a given subsequence.\"\"\"\n        product = 1\n        for num in subsequence:\n            product *= num\n        return product\n\n    def _find_increasing_subsequences(arr_inner: list[int]) -> list[list[int]]:\n        \"\"\"Identifies and returns all increasing subsequences of length two or more.\"\"\"\n        all_subsequences = []\n\n        def generate(start_index: int, current_subsequence: list[int]):\n            # If the current subsequence has at least two elements, it's a valid candidate\n            if len(current_subsequence) >= 2:\n                all_subsequences.append(list(current_subsequence)) # Add a copy to avoid modification issues\n\n            # Explore options for extending the current subsequence\n            for i in range(start_index, len(arr_inner)):\n                # Check if arr_inner[i] can extend the current_subsequence (must be strictly greater)\n                if not current_subsequence or arr_inner[i] > current_subsequence[-1]:\n                    current_subsequence.append(arr_inner[i])\n                    generate(i + 1, current_subsequence)\n                    current_subsequence.pop() # Backtrack: remove last element to explore other paths\n\n        generate(0, [])\n        return all_subsequences\n\n    if not arr:\n        return 0\n\n    max_prod = 0\n    # Use the nested helper function to find all increasing subsequences\n    all_increasing_subsequences = _find_increasing_subsequences(arr)\n\n    # Iterate through each valid subsequence and calculate its product\n    for subsequence in all_increasing_subsequences:\n        current_product = _calculate_product(subsequence)\n        max_prod = max(max_prod, current_product)\n\n    return max_prod"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/473", "solution": "def _get_canonical_tuple(input_tuple: tuple) -> tuple:\n    \"\"\"Converts a given tuple into its canonical form by sorting its elements.\"\"\"\n    return tuple(sorted(input_tuple))\n\ndef tuple_intersection(list1: list[tuple], list2: list[tuple]) -> set[tuple]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the order-agnostic intersection of two lists of tuples,\n    returning a set of canonical tuples.\n    \"\"\"\n    canonical_set1 = {_get_canonical_tuple(t) for t in list1}\n    canonical_set2 = {_get_canonical_tuple(t) for t in list2}\n\n    return canonical_set1.intersection(canonical_set2)"}
