{"task_id": "Mbpp/598", "solution": "def armstrong_number(number: int) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"\n    Checks if the given number is an Armstrong number.\n    An Armstrong number is equal to the sum of its own digits, each raised to the power of the number of digits.\n    \"\"\"\n    if number < 0:\n        return False # Business Rule BR2: non-negative integer.\n\n    num_str = str(number)\n    n = len(num_str)\n    sum_of_powers = 0\n    temp_number = number\n\n    while temp_number > 0:\n        digit = temp_number % 10\n        sum_of_powers += digit ** n\n        temp_number //= 10\n\n    return sum_of_powers == number"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/592", "solution": "# Helper function to calculate factorial\ndef _factorial(x: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"Calculates the factorial of a non-negative integer.\"\"\"\n    if x < 0:\n        # Factorial is typically defined for non-negative integers.\n        # Returning 0 for negative input as per context if it occurs.\n        return 0 \n    if x == 0 or x == 1:\n        return 1\n    \n    result = 1\n    for i in range(2, x + 1):\n        result *= i\n    return result\n\n# Helper function to calculate binomial coefficient C(n, k)\ndef _binomial_coefficient(n: int, k: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"Calculates the binomial coefficient C(n, k) = n! / (k! * (n-k)!).\"\"\"\n    if k < 0 or k > n:\n        return 0\n    \n    # The design explicitly states _binomial_coefficient uses _factorial.\n    return _factorial(n) // (_factorial(k) * _factorial(n - k))\n\ndef sum_Of_product(n: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"Calculates the sum of the product of consecutive binomial coefficients.\"\"\"\n    # For n <= 0, the range(n) loop will be empty, and total_sum will remain 0,\n    # which is the correct sum for an empty series or for n=0.\n    \n    total_sum = 0\n    for k in range(n): # Loop from k=0 to n-1\n        coeff_k = _binomial_coefficient(n, k)\n        coeff_k_plus_1 = _binomial_coefficient(n, k + 1)\n        total_sum += coeff_k * coeff_k_plus_1\n    return total_sum"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/594", "solution": "def is_even(number: int) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"Checks if a given integer is even.\"\"\"\n    return number % 2 == 0\n\ndef is_odd(number: int) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"Checks if a given integer is odd.\"\"\"\n    return number % 2 != 0\n\ndef diff_even_odd(numbers: list[int]) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the difference of the first even and first odd number in a given list.\n    It iterates through the list once to find both numbers efficiently.\n    \"\"\"\n    first_even = None\n    first_odd = None\n\n    for num in numbers:\n        if first_even is None and is_even(num):\n            first_even = num\n        if first_odd is None and is_odd(num):\n            first_odd = num\n        \n        # If both are found, we can stop searching\n        if first_even is not None and first_odd is not None:\n            break\n            \n    return first_even - first_odd"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/597", "solution": "def find_kth(array1: list, array2: list, k: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"Finds the k-th smallest element from two given sorted arrays.\n    This function uses a recursive binary search approach to efficiently find the k-th element.\n    \"\"\"\n    len1, len2 = len(array1), len(array2)\n\n    # Ensure array1 is the shorter array for optimized recursion depth and base case handling.\n    if len1 > len2:\n        return find_kth(array2, array1, k)\n\n    # Base case: if array1 is empty, the k-th element must be in array2.\n    if len1 == 0:\n        return array2[k - 1]\n\n    # Base case: if k is 1, return the minimum of the first elements of both arrays.\n    if k == 1:\n        return min(array1[0], array2[0])\n\n    # Determine how many elements to \"discard\" from each array.\n    # We consider `pa` elements from array1 and `pb` elements from array2, such that `pa + pb = k`.\n    # `pa` is limited by `k // 2` and the actual length of array1.\n    pa = min(k // 2, len1)\n    pb = k - pa\n\n    # Compare the (pa-1)-th element of array1 with the (pb-1)-th element of array2.\n    if array1[pa - 1] <= array2[pb - 1]:\n        # If array1[pa-1] is smaller or equal, it means array1[0...pa-1] cannot contain the k-th element,\n        # unless array1[pa-1] itself is the k-th element. All these `pa` elements (and possibly more)\n        # are definitely smaller than or equal to array2[pb-1].\n        # We discard `pa` elements from array1 and search for the (k - pa)-th element in the remaining parts.\n        return find_kth(array1[pa:], array2, k - pa)\n    else:\n        # If array2[pb-1] is smaller, we discard `pb` elements from array2.\n        # These `pb` elements are guaranteed to be smaller than the k-th element.\n        # We search for the (k - pb)-th element in array1 and the remaining part of array2.\n        return find_kth(array1, array2[pb:], k - pb)"}
